  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933939/assets/5add9f877dc2e93b1c8e562794aa64c83d180f5c683735055ee24f8a190e8740.png)\n\nThe equation of the line ${l}_{1}$ is $y=-4x-3$ and ${l}_{2}$ is perpendicular to ${l}_{1}.$  What is the equation of ${l}_{2}?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Since perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $-4,$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=\\frac{1}{4}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-4) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{1}{4}x-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
